= Coriander Seeds (Dhānyaka)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — dhānyaka is one of the most-cited spice-drugs of the saṃhitās and the canonical_ Pitta-pacifying digestive aromatic_, distinguished from the warming jīraka by its cooling character. Chinese: classical — 胡荽 (húsuī) and 芫荽 (yánsuī) are in the Bencao tradition, with the prefix 胡 (hú) indicating its trans-Iranian / "barbarian" import origin (Coriandrum is West Asian and reached China via the Silk Road). Greco-Roman: fully classical — koríannon is in Dioscorides, Hippocrates, Pliny, the Hebrew Bible (Exodus 16:31, the famous "like coriander seed" simile for manna), and continuous European pharmacy. Modern: ubiquitous Indian, Middle Eastern, Mexican, Thai, European herb / spice. Note: the_ leaf _(cilantro / Chinese parsley) and the_ seed _are pharmacologically distinct drug-forms of the same plant._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Coriandrum sativum_ L., family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae). The plant supplies *two distinct drug-forms*:
- *The seed* (botanically the dried fruit; the dehiscent schizocarp) — sweet, citrus, faintly-floral aroma, used as a *spice*; the principal pharmacological "coriander" in Ayurveda and most classical pharmacy.
- *The leaf* (the fresh young leaves and stems) — pungent, soapy-citrus to some, intensely aromatic — *coriander leaf* in British/Australian English, *cilantro* in American English / Spanish, *Chinese parsley* in some Anglo-Chinese contexts.
The two drug-forms have *different chemistries* and *different cross-cultural names* and reception — this is a critical disambiguation. *This entry focuses on coriander seed* (dhānyaka / 胡荽子 / koríannon); the fresh leaf has its own pharmacological register.
_Coriandrum_ from Greek κορίαννον / κορίανδρον (_kóriannon / koríandron_), from κόρις (_kóris_, "bug, bedbug") — the *unripe coriander fruit* is said by ancient Greek authors to have a "bug-like" smell, which becomes the *sweet citrus-floral* of the dried ripe seed. The "bug-smell" etymology is the classical and persistent gloss; some moderns dispute it but the Liddell-Scott etymology stands.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _kóriannon_, _kóris_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_; Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _coriandrum_). The kóris ("bug") connection is the classical-etymological consensus; the unripe-seed bug-smell is real and is the basis of the popular soap-cilantro-aversion modern observation about the leaf (which is a separate organoleptic phenomenon driven by genetic variation in OR6A2 receptor sensitivity to long-chain aldehydes).]

*English:* _Coriander_ (UK) / coriander seed; *Cilantro* (US, from Spanish) usually refers specifically to the leaf. The two-name split between leaf and seed in American English is a real source of cross-Atlantic confusion.

*Sanskrit:* धन्याका / धान्यक (_dhānyaka_, _dhanyāka_), "of the grain" — from _dhānya_ ("grain, cereal") — the small seeds resembling grain-like fruits. Synonyms: कुस्तुम्बरु (_kustumbaru_), छत्रा (_chatrā_, "umbrella-shaped" — referring to the umbel), धान्या (_dhānyā_), वितुन्नक (_vitunnaka_).

*Hebrew (biblical):* _gad_ (גַּד) — Exodus 16:31 and Numbers 11:7: "And the manna was like coriander seed" (וְהַמָּן כְּזֶרַע גַּד) — the canonical biblical simile for the appearance of manna in the wilderness. The Greek Septuagint translates as κορίον (_kórion_).

*Chinese:* 胡荽 (húsuī) — the canonical name, with prefix 胡 (hú, "foreign / barbarian / from the western regions") indicating the trans-Iranian / Silk Road import origin. 芫荽 (yánsuī) — alternative name; same plant. 胡荽子 (húsuīzǐ) — the seed specifically; 芫荽葉 (yánsuīyè) — the leaf. The 胡 prefix is widely used in Chinese pharmacy for plants of Iranian or Central-Asian origin (cf. 胡桃 húdao = walnut; 胡椒 hújiāo = pepper; 胡麻 húmá = sesame in some classical usages); coriander's Chinese name preserves the trade-route etymology in plain sight.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the 胡 prefix as Silk Road provenance marker is standard Chinese pharmacy etymology — cf. Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

胡荽 (mostly the seed and leaf together) is documented in _Bencao Gangmu_.

*Property:* Warm (溫). *Flavor:* Pungent (辛). *Channels:* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).

*Actions:*
- 發汗透疹 — promotes sweating and brings out skin rashes (the canonical *measles-bringing-out* use: 透疹 transmits the rash to the surface in early measles, supporting the eruption rather than suppressing it).
- 健胃消食 — strengthens the stomach and disperses food stagnation.
- 解表 — releases the exterior (mild diaphoretic for early wind-cold).

*Cautions:* heat-pattern conditions, blazing fire, late-stage measles (where the 透疹 action is no longer indicated).#footnote[胡荽 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; the 透疹 measles-eruption use is well-documented in Chinese pediatric pharmacy.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Dhānyaka is one of the most-cited spice-drugs in the saṃhitās, and the canonical *Pitta-pacifying* digestive aromatic — distinguishing it from cumin (jīraka), which is Vāta/Kapha-pacifying and warming. The two are the two great cooking-spice digestives of the Indian tradition.#footnote[Dhānyaka in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; standard nighaṇṭus. Translations: Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_ (Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa:* Madhura (sweet — characteristic and the basis of the Pitta-pacifying), Kaṭu (pungent), Tikta (bitter), Kaṣāya (astringent) — *four of six rasas*, which is a distinctive multi-rasa profile.
*Guṇa:* Laghu (light), Snigdha (unctuous), Tīkṣṇa (sharp — mild).
*Vīrya:* *Śīta (cooling)* — *the distinguishing feature relative to cumin's uṣṇa*. Coriander is the *cooling* digestive aromatic of Indian cuisine.
*Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of dhānyaka: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; the śīta vīrya is the distinctive feature.]

*Doṣa:* *Tridoṣa-pacifying (tridoṣa-śāmaka)* — particularly pacifies Pitta; gentle on Vāta (the snigdha-madhura) and Kapha (the laghu-tīkṣṇa with cooling). The tridoṣa profile is uncommon and is the basis of coriander's near-universal applicability in Indian cooking.

*Karma:*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles digestion (gentler than cumin).
- _Trṣṇā-nigrahaṇa, dāha-praśamana_ — quenches thirst, relieves burning sensations — the Pitta-pacifying register; classical use in *amlapitta* (hyperacidity, heat reflux).
- _Mūtrala, mūtra-virajanīya_ — mild diuretic and urinary-clearing.
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — eye-supportive.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Jvara-ghna_ — for fever, especially Pitta-fever (the cooling-aromatic register).
- _Garbhāśaya-balya_ — uterine-supportive.
- *Coriander-water (dhānyaka-pānī)* — fresh / soaked-overnight coriander seed water; the canonical Indian Pitta-pacifying digestive drink; effective in heat-related dyspepsia, hyperacidity, summer heat.
- Used in: _amlapitta_, summer heat, Pitta-pattern dyspepsia, Pitta-pattern eye disorders, mild fever, urinary disorders.

=== Viruddha

- *(M) Modern:* generally very safe; Apiaceae allergy; coriander-essential-oil at high doses can be sedating; pregnancy at high-essential-oil doses (culinary safe).

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Coriander is canonical in Tibb — the Pitta-pacifying digestive aromatic of the Arabic tradition, paralleling its Ayurvedic placement. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* كزبرة (_kuzbara_) — seed (_kuzbarat al-bizr_) and leaf (_kuzbara khaḍrā'_) distinguished. Persian: گشنیز (_geshnīz_). Urdu/Hakeemi: دھنیا (_dhaniyā_, from Sanskrit _dhānyaka_) and کشنیز (_kishnīz_).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* not Qur'ānic; some Tibb al-Nabawī references in the dietetic register.

*Mizāj:* the unusual feature is the *seed-vs-leaf distinction*. The *seed* is *Hot and Dry in the 1st degree* (some sources balanced); the *leaf* is *Cold and Dry* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kuzbara_). The seed is digestive-warming; the leaf is cooling. The seed's mild-warming classification *diverges from the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya* of dhānyaka — a real cross-system divergence on the seed; the leaf classification converges.

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-mi'da*; *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative); *muḥaffif li-l-warm* (anti-inflammatory); *mufariḥ* (mild mood-elevating); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *jālī* (cleansing); the leaf *mubarriḍ* (cooling) and useful for *amrāḍ ṣafrāwiyya*.

*Istemālāt:* dyspepsia; flatulence; mild palpitations from heat; nausea; nasal congestion (topical with onion or rose-water); urinary disorders; the leaf for hot-pattern conditions and *amrāḍ ṣafrāwiyya*.

*Mawāne':* the seed for cold-pattern conditions of phlegmatic constitution in moderation; the leaf for cold-pattern dyspepsia.

*Muṣliḥ:* sugar for the seed; ginger for the leaf.

*Badal:* fennel or cumin for the seed; mint for the leaf.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sufūf-i Kishnīz* (coriander seed powder for dyspepsia); *Joshānda-yi Kuzbara* (decoction for diuretic and digestive uses); *Sharbat-i Kishnīz* (cooling sherbet, especially for summer heat); the seed appears in many Unani digestive compound powders alongside cumin and fennel. The classical Persian *kuzbara-with-rose-water* preparation is a foundational cooling drink.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kuzbara_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _dhaniyā / kishnīz_ entry.#footnote[Tibb kuzbara: classical Avicennan reading with seed-leaf distinction. The seed-mizāj divergence from Ayurvedic śīta vīrya is one of the standing cross-system disagreements.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *胡荽湯* — coriander decoction for measles-eruption support; classical Chinese pediatric pharmacy.
- *Topical coriander preparations* for skin rashes.

=== Ayurveda

- *Dhānyaka cūrṇa* — simple powder; 1–3 g for digestive support.
- *Dhānyaka-pānī (coriander-water)* — soaked coriander seed water; cooling digestive drink.
- *Dhānyakādi cūrṇa, Pañca-kola cūrṇa* — compound digestive powders including coriander.
- *Tadkā / chhonk* — alongside cumin in nearly every Indian dish.
- *Hingvāṣṭaka and other compound digestives* — coriander as supportive ingredient.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

- *Dioscorides* (III.64, _kóriannon_) treats coriander as cooling and astringent — *converging with the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya* — useful for inflammation, stomach, sleep (in small doses; large doses are warned against as "disturbing the mind").
- *Pliny* (_N.H._ XX.82) gives a long account.
- *Roman cookery (Apicius)* uses coriander seed extensively.
- *Hebrew Bible*: Exodus 16:31 — "the manna was like coriander seed, white" — the canonical biblical simile.
- *Medieval European*: continued use; in monastic medicine and the Salernitan corpus.
- *Hildegard*: mentions coriander.
- *Galenic / pharmacy preparations*: _Aqua coriandri_, _Pulvis coriandri_, _Coriander confects_ (sugar-coated seed-comfits, a major medieval European confection — the ancestor of modern sugar-coated comfits).
- *Modern Western*: coriander seed in pickling spice, sausage spice (esp. Polish kiełbasa); the leaf (cilantro) in Mexican / Vietnamese / Thai cuisine.
- *Mexican use*: cilantro leaf is foundational to modern Mexican cuisine.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.64 (Beck); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XX.82 (Loeb); Apicius. The biblical reference: Exodus 16:31, Numbers 11:7. Medieval: Salernitan corpus and standard pharmacy histories; coriander comfits in medieval / early-modern European confectionery.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (seed):*
- *Volatile oil (0.2–1.5% of dried seed):* dominated by *linalool* (~60–80%) — the characteristic sweet floral note — with *α-pinene*, *γ-terpinene*, *p-cymene*, *camphor*, *geraniol*, *geranyl acetate*.
- *Fixed oil ~20%*.
- Flavonoids; phenolic acids; coumarins.
- Vitamin C in fresh; minerals.

*Leaf (cilantro)* has a distinctly different chemistry — dominated by *long-chain aldehydes* (E-2-decenal and related) — which are the basis of the *soapy aversion* reaction in some 4–14% of the population, linked to genetic variation at the OR6A2 olfactory receptor.

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Carminative / antispasmodic*: well-documented; the Ayurvedic-Dioscoridean classical convergence.
- *Anxiolytic / sedative (mild)*: linalool has anxiolytic activity (well-characterized in lavender pharmacology where linalool is also the principal compound).
- *Hypolipidemic, glycemic*: modest clinical evidence in type-2 diabetes.
- *Antioxidant, antimicrobial*.
- *Heavy-metal chelation*: *cilantro leaf* has documented chelation activity (preclinical) for lead, mercury, and cadmium — the basis of the popular "cilantro detox" wellness claim; clinical translation is *modest*.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Ayurvedic śīta-cooling digestive + Dioscoridean cooling stomachic + modern carminative-anxiolytic-linalool pharmacology converge.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use*: up to ~3 g/day in cooking; well-tolerated.
- *Ayurvedic medicinal*: 1–3 g/day powder; 60–120 mL coriander-water decoction.
- *Risk groups*: Apiaceae allergy; pregnancy at high-essential-oil doses (culinary safe); high doses of essential oil can be sedating.

